/-!
# Potential geometrically reduced normalizations

Source: Anantharaman, Appendix II, Corollary 2, printed p.73.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-semitrait-potential-reduced-normalization).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- The componentwise normalization of the reduction of the specified base change.
Source: Anantharaman, Appendix II, Corollary 2, printed p.73.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-semitrait-potential-reduced-normalization). -/
noncomputable def semitraitNormalization {R : Type u} [CommRing R]
    {Z : Scheme.{u}} (f : Z ⟶ Spec (.of R)) (L : Type u) [Field L] [Algebra R L] :
    Scheme.{u} :=
  Scheme.reducedNormalization (Scheme.IdealSheafData.subscheme
    (Scheme.nilradical (pullback (C := Scheme.{u}) f
      (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap R (integralClosure R L)))))))

/-- The canonical normalization map, followed by the reduction immersion.
Source: Anantharaman, Appendix II, Corollary 2, printed p.73.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-semitrait-potential-reduced-normalization). -/
noncomputable def semitraitNormalizationMap {R : Type u} [CommRing R]
    {Z : Scheme.{u}} (f : Z ⟶ Spec (.of R)) (L : Type u) [Field L] [Algebra R L] :
    semitraitNormalization f L ⟶
      pullback (C := Scheme.{u}) f
        (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap R (integralClosure R L)))) :=
  Scheme.fromReducedNormalization (Scheme.IdealSheafData.subscheme
    (Scheme.nilradical (pullback (C := Scheme.{u}) f
      (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap R (integralClosure R L))))))) ≫
  Scheme.IdealSheafData.subschemeι (Scheme.nilradical (pullback (C := Scheme.{u}) f
    (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap R (integralClosure R L))))))

/-- The structure map of the normalization to the spectrum of the integral closure.
Source: Anantharaman, Appendix II, Corollary 2, printed p.73.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-semitrait-potential-reduced-normalization). -/
noncomputable def semitraitNormalizationStructure {R : Type u} [CommRing R]
    {Z : Scheme.{u}} (f : Z ⟶ Spec (.of R)) (L : Type u) [Field L] [Algebra R L] :
    semitraitNormalization f L ⟶ Spec (.of (integralClosure R L)) :=
  semitraitNormalizationMap f L ≫
    pullback.snd f (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap R (integralClosure R L))))

/-- A simultaneous finite extension gives the canonical normalizations reduced fibres.
Source: Anantharaman, Appendix II, Corollary 2, printed p.73.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-semitrait-potential-reduced-normalization).

Proof plan from the node (no part is proved at stage 2):

1. Choose an algebraic closure $\Omega/K$. Index by the finite intermediate fields $K\subseteq
F\subseteq\Omega$, a small nonempty directed set: the field generated by finitely many algebraic
elements is finite by [lem-weil-pbc-algebraic-generators-finite-extension]. Write $R_F$ for the
integral closure of $R$ in $F$, and $B$ for its integral closure in $\Omega$. Every $b\in B$
belongs to some finite $F$, and its monic equation over $R$ is the same equation in $F$. Thus
$B=\bigcup_FR_F=\varinjlim_FR_F$: compatible ring maps on these subrings glue by their common-
stage formula. The affine schemes $S_F=\operatorname{Spec}R_F$ have limit $\operatorname{Spec}B$
by [lem-weil-pbc-limit-affine-transition].

2. For every finite $F/K$, [lem-sc-integral-closure-dedekind-surjective-fibres] makes $R_F$ a
Noetherian normal domain of dimension one, with fraction field $F$ by [lem-weil-pbc-integral-
closure-algebraic-extension-normal]. Also $\operatorname{Frac}B=\Omega$. The inclusion
$R_F\subseteq B\subseteq\Omega$ is torsion-free, hence flat by [lem-sfb-dedekind-torsionfree-
module-flat]. It is integral (the monic equations over $R$ are also equations over $R_F$), so
lying over and [lem-weil-pbc-flat-surjective-spectrum-faithfully-flat] make it faithfully flat. No
finite-module assertion about $R_F/R$ has entered.

3. Choose a finite affine open cover of each $Z_i$, finitely many charts altogether since $I$ is
finite and the morphisms are of finite type. Write a chart as $U=\operatorname{Spec}A$. Its
algebra is finite type over $R$ by [lem-weil-pbc-lft-affine-pairs], flat by [lem-weil-pbc-flat-
morphism-affine-criterion], and finitely presented over $R$ by [lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-
noetherian-finite-presentation]. Its generic fibre is geometrically normal: after any field
extension it is an open subscheme of the given geometrically normal generic fibre, so its local
rings have the stated property. For $A_F=A\otimes_RR_F$, base change preserves flatness and finite
type. The algebra $A_F$ is Noetherian since it is finite type over $R_F$. It is reduced: flatness
injects $A_F$ into $A\otimes_RF$, and that generic algebra is normal and therefore reduced.
Explicitly, a nilpotent in $A_F$ maps to zero in the generic algebra, so injectivity kills it.

4. For each chart $A$, apply [lem-sfb-absolute-dedekind-normalization-reduced-fibres] to
$R,A,\Omega$. All its hypotheses were verified in Step 3. It supplies the concrete integral
closure $C\subseteq A_K\otimes_K\Omega$: $C$ is finite over $A_B$, flat and finitely presented
over $B$, normal, with reduced geometric fibres and the specified generic-fibre isomorphism. This
is the infinite algebraic-closure stage of Raynaud's argument, before choosing a finite field
extension.

5. By [lem-kun-fp-algebra-descent], descend $C$ as a finitely presented algebra $C_{F_0}$ over
some $R_{F_0}$. The algebra $A_{F_0}$ is finitely presented, since it is finite type over the
Noetherian ring $R_{F_0}$. Apply [lem-weil-pbc-colimit-category-fp-algebras] to descend the
$B$-algebra map $A_B\to C$ to an $R_F$-algebra map $A_F\to C_F$ at a later stage. The prescribed
comparison $C_F\otimes_{R_F}B\cong C$ identifies the base change of this map with the original
map. All subsequent enlargements mean base change of these fixed models; the finite set of charts
permits a common stage.

6. Enlarge $F$ so $A_F\to C_F$ is finite by [lem-weil-pbc-colimit-finite-ring-map], and $R_F\to
C_F$ is flat by [lem-weil-pbc-colimit-flat-ring-map]. The first applies because $C_F$ is already
finitely generated over $R_F$, hence over $A_F$; the second because $C_F$ is finitely presented
over $R_F$. Apply [lem-trf-geometrically-reduced-fibres-limit-descent] to
$\operatorname{Spec}C_F\to\operatorname{Spec}R_F$ and the system $S_E$, $E\supseteq F$. The base
schemes are affine qcqs, transitions affine, and the displayed morphism is of finite presentation.
Its limit has reduced geometric fibres by Step 4. Hence enlarge again so this holds over $R_F$.
Finite generation of the module persists by tensoring a finite generating family; flatness
persists by flat base change. In particular these properties persist under further base change, in
particular geometric reducedness by [lem-trf-geometric-reduced-fibre-locus-base-change].

7. The map $\operatorname{Spec}(C_F\otimes_{R_F}F)\to\operatorname{Spec}(A_K\otimes_KF)$ is of
finite presentation: both algebras are finite type over the field, hence Noetherian, and the
source algebra is finitely generated over the target algebra. Its base change to $\Omega$ is the
generic-fibre isomorphism of Step 4. The fields $E\supseteq F$ have union $\Omega$, so their
spectra have limit $\operatorname{Spec}\Omega$. Apply [lem-sfb-scheme-limit-isomorphism-descent]
to this inverse system to enlarge $F$ until the generic map itself is an isomorphism. Finite
presentation over a field is supplied by [lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-noetherian-finite-
presentation]; all sources and targets here are affine qcqs.

8. The ring map $C_F\to C_F\otimes_{R_F}B\cong C$ is faithfully flat by Step 2 and [lem-weil-pbc-
faithfully-flat-base-change]. Since $C$ is normal, [lem-sfb-faithfully-flat-normal-ring-descent]
proves that $C_F$ is normal. This remains true for every later stage: the map from that stage to
the same ring $C$ is still faithfully flat. Thus the final common field may be chosen after all
the preceding conditions for every chart, without any normality-persistence assumption under
arbitrary base change.

9. Recognize $\operatorname{Spec}C_F$ as the componentwise normalization of
$\operatorname{Spec}A_F$. Both are locally Noetherian, since $A_F$ and $C_F$ are finite type over
$R_F$. Both are flat over $R_F$. By [lem-sc-flat-going-down], every minimal prime contracts to
$(0)$: a prime properly above $(0)$ would admit a smaller prime by going down. Their component
generic points therefore lie in the generic fibre. The generic-fibre isomorphism of Step 7
identifies these points bijectively and identifies their residue fields. The finite map is
integral by [lem-weil-pbc-finite-algebra-is-integral], and the source is normal by Step 8. [lem-
sfb-integral-normal-generic-recognition], applied to the locally Noetherian scheme
$\operatorname{Spec}A_F$, now gives its unique isomorphism with the componentwise normalization
over $\operatorname{Spec}A_F$.

10. Fix the one common finite stage $L$ just obtained for all charts of all $Z_i$. Set
$T=\operatorname{Spec}R_L$ and $X_i=Z_i\times_ST$. Each $X_i$ is reduced by the generic-
injectivity argument of Step 3; hence its reduction is itself. Each $X_i$ is Noetherian by [lem-
weil-pbc-finite-type-over-noetherian], so its quasi-compact opens have finitely many irreducible
components by [lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-finitely-many-components]. By [lem-sfb-normalization-
generic-residue-affine-charts], applied both to $X_i$ and to the affine chart
$U_T=\operatorname{Spec}A_L$ itself (same generic points and residue fields), the restriction of
$N_i=(X_{i,\mathrm{red}})^\nu$ to $U_T$ is $\operatorname{Spec}D_{U_T}$, i.e. the componentwise
normalization of $U_T$, which is the one recognized in Step 9. It is therefore finite there, flat
over $R_L$, and has geometrically reduced fibres. Finiteness is local for the Zariski covering of
the target, as an instance of [lem-weil-pbc-fpqc-descend-finite]; this open cover is an fpqc
covering. Flatness follows from [lem-weil-pbc-flat-morphism-affine-criterion]. Reducedness of a
geometric fibre is checked on these open affine covers by [lem-weil-pbc-affine-scheme-reduced-
iff]. By [lem-trf-affine-geometric-fibre-reduced-comparison], reducedness of the geometric fibre
of a chart at $\mathfrak p$ means that its fibre over $\kappa(\mathfrak p)$ is geometrically
reduced; a fibre over any field $\kappa(\mathfrak p)\to E$ is that fibre tensored with $E$. Thus
every field-valued fibre is geometrically reduced, including empty fibres.

11. Each $N_i$ is normal and its canonical map to $X_i$ is surjective by [lem-sfb-componentwise-
normalization-generic-properties], since $X_i$ is locally Noetherian (Step 10). The maps are
finite by Step 10. Base change makes $X_i\to T$ separated and of finite type by [lem-weil-pbc-
separated-base-change] and [lem-sfb-finite-type-composition-base-change]; [lem-weil-pbc-finite-
over-separated-finite-type] therefore makes $N_i\to T$ separated and of finite type. The chartwise
generic isomorphisms of Step 7 are the canonical normalization maps by Step 9 and hence give the
claimed global generic-fibre isomorphism. If the family is empty or all charts are empty, take
$L=K$; all conclusions are vacuous on empty schemes. Charts with empty special fibre require no
change in the argument. This completes every clause with a single $L$, without excellence or
module-finiteness of $R_L/R$.
-/
theorem exists_finite_extension_semitrait_normalization
    (R : Type u) [CommRing R] [IsDomain R] [IsNoetherianRing R] [IsIntegrallyClosed R]
    [Finite (MaximalSpectrum R)] (hdim : ringKrullDim R = 1)
    (K : Type u) [Field K] [Algebra R K] [IsFractionRing R K]
    (I : Type v) [Finite I] (Z : I → Scheme.{u})
    (f : ∀ i, Z i ⟶ Spec (.of R))
    [∀ i, Flat (f i)] [∀ i, LocallyOfFiniteType (f i)] [∀ i, QuasiCompact (f i)]
    [∀ i, IsSeparated (f i)]
    (hgeom : ∀ (i : I) (E : Type u) [Field E] [Algebra K E],
      ∀ x : pullback (C := Scheme.{u})
        (pullback.snd (f i) (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap R K))))
        (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap K E))),
        IsDomain ((pullback (C := Scheme.{u})
          (pullback.snd (f i) (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap R K))))
          (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap K E)))).presheaf.stalk x) ∧
        IsIntegrallyClosed ((pullback (C := Scheme.{u})
          (pullback.snd (f i) (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap R K))))
          (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap K E)))).presheaf.stalk x)) :
    ∃ (L : Type u) (_ : Field L) (_ : Algebra K L) (_ : Algebra R L),
      IsScalarTower R K L ∧ FiniteDimensional K L ∧
      ∀ i : I,
        IsFinite (semitraitNormalizationMap (f i) L) ∧
        Surjective (semitraitNormalizationMap (f i) L) ∧
        (∀ x : semitraitNormalization (f i) L,
          IsDomain ((semitraitNormalization (f i) L).presheaf.stalk x) ∧
          IsIntegrallyClosed ((semitraitNormalization (f i) L).presheaf.stalk x)) ∧
        Flat (semitraitNormalizationStructure (f i) L) ∧
        IsSeparated (semitraitNormalizationStructure (f i) L) ∧
        LocallyOfFiniteType (semitraitNormalizationStructure (f i) L) ∧
        QuasiCompact (semitraitNormalizationStructure (f i) L) ∧
        GeometricallyReduced (semitraitNormalizationStructure (f i) L) ∧
        IsIso (((Over.pullback
          (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap (integralClosure R L) L)))).map
          (Over.homMk
            (U := Over.mk (semitraitNormalizationStructure (f i) L))
            (V := Over.mk (pullback.snd (f i)
              (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap R (integralClosure R L))))))
            (semitraitNormalizationMap (f i) L) rfl)).left) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
